            <ArticleTitle>Subcutaneous versus intracutaneous injections of sterile water for labour analgesia: a comparison of perceived pain during administration.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1248-51</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To investigate whether, during injections of sterile water, there is any difference in perceived pain between intracutaneous and subcutaneous injections.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Blind controlled trial with cross-over design.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Göteborg and Skövde, Sweden.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">One hundred healthy female volunteers.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The women were randomised into two groups and subjected to two trials, within one week of each other. During the first trial one group (n = 50) received the intracutaneous injection first, followed by the subcutaneous injection. The second group (n = 50) was given the subcutaneous injection first, followed by intracutaneous injection. In both groups all the injections were given in reverse order during the second trial.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Experienced pain during the administration of sterile water injections, measured by visual analogue scale.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The analysis showed intracutaneous injections to be significantly more painful than subcutaneous injections, even after adjusting for injection day and for left/right site of injection (mean 60.8 vs 41.3, P &lt; 0.001).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The findings suggest that the less painful subcutaneous injection technique should be used.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
